<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léon M'ba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Léon Mba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léon M'ba was a Gabonese politician who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> to independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 17 August 1960 and became its first president. He kept close economic and military ties with France and built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>. In 1964 a military coup briefly removed him, but French troops restored him to power. He died in office in 1967 and was succeeded by Omar Bongo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leon-mba.jpg" alt="Léon M'ba" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9on_M%27ba_1964.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Léon M'ba was born on 9 February 1902 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a>, a member of the Fang ethnic group. His father was a small business manager and village chief who had once worked as hairdresser to the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza</a>, and his mother, Louise Bendome, was a seamstress. He joined a seminary in 1909, then worked as a store manager, lumberjack and trader before entering the French colonial administration as a customs agent. His activism for Black Gabonese, particularly the Fang, worried his superiors, and in December 1922 he was sentenced to prison for giving a colleague falsified documents.</p><p>In the mid-1920s he was reconciled with the authorities and made chef de canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a>'s Fang neighbourhood. Accused of complicity in the murder of a woman near Libreville, he was sentenced in 1931 to three years in prison and ten years in exile in Oubangui-Chari. There he wrote an essay on the customary law of the Fang, published in 1938, which became the main reference on the subject. He was allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> in 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After his return M'ba founded the Gabonese Mixed Committee, was elected to the Territorial Assembly in 1952 and became mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a> in 1956. On 21 May 1957 he was appointed vice-president of the governor's council, the highest position then held by a native African in French Gabon. He became the first prime minister of Gabon in 1959. In the month before independence he signed fifteen cooperation agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, covering national defence, technical cooperation, economic support and national stability, and on 17 August 1960 independence was proclaimed.</p><p>In February 1961 he was elected president as the only candidate, and a new constitution that month gave him full executive powers. A cult of personality developed around him, with songs in his praise and stamps and loincloths bearing his image. On 17 and 18 February 1964 about 150 soldiers arrested him and announced a coup; a provisional government was offered to Jean-Hilaire Aubame. M'ba was restored within days after French intervention under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, and Aubame was sentenced to ten years of hard labour and ten years of exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1965 the French looked for a successor to the ageing and sick president and chose Albert Bernard Bongo, whom M'ba appointed vice-president in November 1966. M'ba was re-elected on 19 March 1967 with 99.9 per cent of the vote. He died of cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 28 November 1967, and Bongo succeeded him the next day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>'s main airport, Leon M'ba International Airport, was later named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Léon M'ba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1958 Gabon became an autonomous republic within the French Community and gained full independence on 17 August 1960 under <b>Léon M'ba</b>, keeping close economic and military ties with France and Libreville as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon (French colony)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.41,9.44&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Libreville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4812 2148 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Léon M'ba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 2418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=0.41,9.44&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
